      "writtenOn": "2019-01-21T04:06:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agree with point 1 that since this is specifically NATT, it can be called makeNattKeepAlive or createNattKeepAlive. ConnectivityManager has neither make* nor create* methods yet, but ConnectivityService has plenty.\nIt looks to me like we are unfortunately not too consistent within ConnectivityService – the general pattern is make* is for internal use and create* is for external use but I don\u0027t think this is intentional. We do have some strong position that some of the make* are internal, and create* is I think slightly more common.\n\nAll in all, I favor \"createNattKeepalive\".\n\nAs for the start() call, while this makes some sense for NAT-T on UDP sockets, I think this is only the first incarnation of something that will get more common and a start() and stop() method will make sense generally. I don\u0027t think the burden on apps to make themselves a method\n\n  private SocketKeepAlive makeNattKeepalive(n, s, s, d, e, c) {\n    final SocketKeepAlive nka \u003d mConnectivityManager.createNattKeepalive(n, s, s, d, e, c);\n    nka.start();\n    return nka;\n  }\n\n...carries enough to outweigh the consistency argument.",
      "parentUuid": "d6e4e7cd_00a4c4c9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1841,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 1841,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "e73e6238179c1e5133882b7666e71d42d66ca21d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
